/**
 * Creates follow-up tasks for the passed opportunities.
 *
 * This intentionally differs from a per-record insert/try-catch loop.
 * 150-250 individual inserts in one call would exceed the 150 DML statement
 * limit. A single allOrNone=false insert still isolates record-level validation
 * failures, so one rejected task does not prevent the other tasks from being
 * created.
 */
public class FollowUpTaskService {
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Set<Id> uniqueOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }

        if (uniqueOpportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueOpportunityIds
        ];

        if (opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Date activityDate = Date.today().addDays(7);
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();

        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            tasks.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId = opportunity.Id,
                    OwnerId = opportunity.OwnerId,
                    Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opportunity.Name,
                    ActivityDate = activityDate,
                    Status = 'Not Started',
                    Priority = 'Normal'
                )
            );
        }

        if (!tasks.isEmpty()) {
            Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.insert(tasks, false);

            for (Integer index = 0; index < results.size(); index++) {
                if (!results[index].isSuccess()) {
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(tasks[index].WhatId);
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

I do not enqueue one future call per opportunity. The trigger collects the unique affected `AccountId`s and enqueues a single `@future` call per trigger transaction using a comma-separated ID string. That avoids exceeding the 50 future calls per transaction limit when CPQ saves 200 opportunities, and avoids repeating the same work for duplicate accounts. I also recalculate both the old and new account when `AccountId` changes.

